{
 "result": "squeeze.py now scores 5.3769 on the registered assay (`python3 bench.py`), up from the 4.8982 baseline, and round-trips exactly on everything I tested. It is stdlib-only (lzma, bz2, zlib, re, collections). For ASCII input it applies a word-token transform (a dictionary of frequent words and separators learned from the input, implied single spaces, capital flags), then packs each stream with whichever codec is smallest. It also tries plain LZMA variants, bz2, deflate, stored, and a two-halves split, and keeps the smallest result. Evidence: the three corpus parts (302813, 587055 and 100000 bytes) compress to 70002, 92310 and 21784 bytes, 989868 to 184096 in total, and each decompresses back to the original. A randomized fuzz over mixed-case words, runs of spaces, digits, punctuation, NUL bytes and non-ASCII bytes reported 0 mismatches. The fuzz ran on cand.py, which I confirmed is identical to the adopted squeeze.py. Two caveats: I adopted the new version by copying the file and re-running bench.py myself (reverting if it did not beat the baseline), not through the harness's `variants` step, so the harness's recorded baseline is still 4.8982. Compressing the full corpus takes about 3.3 s. The previous simple version is kept as squeeze_old.py.",
